WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.603
Per-model prompt tuning, with equal budgets.

2
00:00:02.883 --> 00:00:13.549
A prompt tuned for months on one model carries that model’s quirks: the phrasing it responds to, the formatting it needs spelled out, the failure modes you patched with extra instructions.

3
00:00:13.829 --> 00:00:19.141
Run it unchanged on another model and you’re comparing a fitted system with an unfitted one.

4
00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:34.547
Each provider publishes its own prompting guide (Anthropic’s prompting best practices, OpenAI’s prompt engineering guide, Google’s prompt design strategies for Gemini), and their advice differs in emphasis, as Studio 1 showed.

5
00:00:34.827 --> 00:00:41.845
The incumbent advantage is real, and it biases most informal comparisons toward whatever the team already uses.

6
00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:44.216
The fix is an equal tuning budget.

7
00:00:44.496 --> 00:00:48.272
Split your questions into a development set and a held-out test set.

8
00:00:48.552 --> 00:01:00.819
Give each candidate the same allowance on the dev set: the same number of prompt iterations, or the same engineer-hours, starting from the same task description and following that provider’s guidance.

9
00:01:01.099 --> 00:01:12.939
Mechanical adaptations (each API’s parameter for the system prompt, its structured-output mechanism, its tool format) don’t count against the budget, because they are porting, not tuning.

10
00:01:13.219 --> 00:01:17.421
Then freeze every configuration and score each once on the test set.

11
00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:19.195
Two rules make this work.

12
00:01:19.475 --> 00:01:30.611
The test set is touched once, at the end; if you iterate on it, the winner is partly the configuration that best fits those particular questions (lesson 7’s GoDeeper on searching).

13
00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:36.693
And record the budget each candidate actually used, so a reader can see the comparison was even.

14
00:01:36.973 --> 00:01:47.875
When a team can’t afford equal tuning, the honest report says “the incumbent prompt, unmodified, on the challenger” and treats a loss as inconclusive rather than as a verdict.
